# MCSP Assembly Dominance and Bootstrap Policy 0.27

Status: Constructive architecture
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parent: Propagation 0.26

## 1. Architectural rule

MCSP is an ASSEMBLY-DOMINANT project.

Assembly-level realization is the primary implementation direction for MCSP.

C is admitted only as a temporary testing stud for bootstrap experiments, qualification tooling, comparison, and reconstruction assistance.

C SHALL NOT become:
- semantic authority;
- the definition of MCSP;
- a required permanent runtime;
- the canonical representation;
- a hidden portability layer;
- a substitute for assembly realization;
- a dependency that MCSP cannot ultimately remove.

## 2. Meaning versus assembly

Assembly dominance does not mean a particular assembler defines MCSP semantics.

The direction remains:

    determinate condition
      -> qualified semantic pre-settlement
      -> normalized scaffold
      -> assembly realization
      -> machine realization
      -> projection
      -> qualification

MCSP meaning is pre-settled above a particular assembly spelling.

Assembly is the dominant realization medium.

## 3. Assembly plurality

MCSP SHALL NOT collapse distinct assembler/ISA boundaries merely for implementation convenience.

Examples:

    x86-64 / NASM
    x86-64 / GAS
    AArch64 / GAS
    RISC-V / GAS

Each is a distinct realization witness where applicable.

A qualified canonical may propagate into each realization branch while assembler spelling, registers, encodings, instruction sequences, and ABI details differ.

## 4. C testing stud

A C implementation may temporarily:
- encode bootstrap scaffold fixtures;
- normalize structural records;
- test renaming invariance;
- test material-order sensitivity;
- test dimension-status sensitivity;
- test propagation;
- inject contradictions;
- verify deterministic reconstruction;
- compare assembly witness projections.

Any result obtained through C is qualification evidence only.

It does not establish that C concepts are MCSP primitives.

## 5. Removal obligation

Every C testing stud SHALL have an explicit removal or replacement path.

For each material C capability, record:

    C_TEST_FUNCTION
    SEMANTIC_OBLIGATION
    ASSEMBLY_REPLACEMENT_TARGET
    QUALIFICATION_REQUIRED
    REMOVAL_STATE

Provisional removal states:

    TEMPORARY
    ASSEMBLY_CANDIDATE
    ASSEMBLY_REIMPLEMENTED
    CROSS_QUALIFIED
    C_REMOVABLE
    REMOVED

These are project/qualification statuses, not MCSP kernel ontology.

## 6. No C leakage

A semantic rule discovered while testing in C must be represented independently of C before it can become an MCSP semantic commitment.

C types, structs, pointers, integer widths, calling conventions, object model, compiler behavior, standard library behavior, and source syntax SHALL NOT silently leak into MCSP semantics.

## 7. Assembly-first implementation order

For executable MCSP development:

    semantic obligation
      -> minimal structural specification
      -> assembly realization design
      -> optional temporary C test stud
      -> assembly implementation
      -> independent comparison
      -> qualification
      -> remove C dependency where qualified

C may accelerate testing but SHALL NOT reverse this authority order.

## 8. Structural bootstrap 0.27 target

The first structural bootstrap implementation SHALL define fixtures independently of documentary names.

Required tests:

    N1 renaming invariance
    N2 irrelevant ordering invariance
    N3 material ordering sensitivity
    N4 OPEN versus PRESET sensitivity
    N5 qualification sensitivity
    N6 unresolved-residue sensitivity

Propagation tests:

    P1 compatible inheritance
    P2 qualified refinement
    P3 explicit narrowing
    P4 contradiction stops affected propagation
    P5 OPEN remains OPEN
    P6 realization-defined branches may diverge
    P7 canonical invariant survives compatible branches
    P8 unresolved residue propagates visibly
    P9 failed qualification never propagates as PASS
    P10 lineage reconstructs deterministically

## 9. First assembly targets

The initial assembly qualification path SHOULD begin with:

    x86-64 NASM

followed independently by:

    x86-64 GAS

Then:

    AArch64 GAS
    RISC-V GAS

The first two provide same-ISA/different-assembler evidence.
The later witnesses provide materially different ISA evidence.

No witness is semantic authority.

## 10. Assembly scaffold propagation

A normalized semantic scaffold may propagate into an assembly branch.

The branch settles only dimensions required for that realization.

Example:

    canonical scaffold
        |
        +-- x86-64 / NASM
        +-- x86-64 / GAS
        +-- AArch64 / GAS
        +-- RISC-V / GAS

OPEN dimensions SHALL remain OPEN until the branch explicitly requires settlement.

## 11. Self-description direction

The long-term target is not:

    English -> C -> MCSP

The target is:

    MCSP-described semantic scaffold
      -> MCSP-qualified assembly realization
      -> machine realization
      -> MCSP-qualified projection

C exists only as temporary construction support while this path is being established.

## 12. Qualification boundary

At 0.27:

Assembly dominance:
    ESTABLISHED as architectural policy.

C:
    TEMPORARY TESTING STUD.

Structural normalizer:
    NOT YET RUNTIME QUALIFIED.

Assembly normalizer:
    NOT YET IMPLEMENTED.

Host independence:
    UNVERIFIED.

Hardware independence:
    UNVERIFIED.

Pre-semantic kernel:
    DETERMINATE CONDITION.

## 13. Next implementation checkpoint

Implement the bootstrap normalization fixtures and comparison contract, then produce the first x86-64 NASM realization.

A C normalizer may be used temporarily as an independent test oracle/stud, but the assembly implementation is the primary implementation artifact and must be capable of qualification without permanent C dependency.
